# Workflow spawn waits were unbounded, so one missing event cost the whole CI job budget (2026-10-05)

== 1. What this batch changes

`session::workflow::manager::tests` had 15 places that waited for a subagent spawn event with
`subagent_rx.recv().await`, i.e. with no deadline at all. The module already contained a bounded
helper, `recv_spawn`, with a 2-second `tokio::time::timeout`, and four tests already used it. This
batch routes all 15 remaining sites through that helper and gives the helper a `what: &str` label so
each site's own wording appears in the panic when the deadline fires. Net diff: 34 insertions,
63 deletions. No production code changed; `recv_spawn` is `#[cfg(test)]` and private to the module.

    $ git diff --stat crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/src/session/workflow/manager.rs
     .../xai-grok-shell/src/session/workflow/manager.rs | 97 ++++++++--------------
     1 file changed, 34 insertions(+), 63 deletions(-)

Four textual shapes were rewritten: `let SubagentEvent::Spawn(..) = recv().await.expect(..) else`,
a recv into a temporary followed by `let else`, a recv pushed straight into a `Vec`, and one
`if let SubagentEvent::Spawn(..) .. else { panic!() }`. The last one lost its `else` branch entirely,
because the helper panics on a non-spawn event with the same message plus the label.

== 2. The CI failure being fixed

CI run 37259667663 on `a8274d9f` ended `cancelled`, consuming exactly the `timeout-minutes: 60`
ceiling at `.github/workflows/ci.yml:33`. The seven other jobs succeeded; the platform legs were
skipped because the Linux job never finished. The job never reported a failure -- it stopped
producing output, which in a CI log is indistinguishable from still running.

    2026-10-05T04:00:01.3117295Z test util::subprocess::tests::post_exit_budget_is_shared_across_streams ... ok
    2026-10-05T04:00:55.2530026Z test session::workflow::manager::tests::active_run_admission_is_bounded_per_session has been running for over 60 seconds
    2026-10-05T04:30:09.2758600Z ##[error]The operation was canceled.
    2026-10-05T04:30:09.6341396Z Terminate orphan process: pid (9174) (cargo)
    2026-10-05T04:30:09.6381062Z Terminate orphan process: pid (103204) (xai_grok_shell-eda4bd4ea2709cff)

libtest's own watchdog named exactly one test, and 29 minutes passed between that line and the
runner killing the processes. Successful runs of the same job had taken 39, 41, 44, 45 and 45
minutes, so the ceiling itself is not normally the constraint.

The same run shows the machine degrading rather than one test being unlucky: an unrelated test also
crossed the watchdog and then recovered.

    2026-10-05T03:53:59.7401144Z test plan_approval_restored_after_resume has been running for over 60 seconds
    2026-10-05T03:56:10.4114751Z test plan_approval_restored_after_resume ... ok

That is 2 minutes 11 seconds for a test that normally finishes in well under one.

== 3. Which await was unbounded, and why the event can be missing

`active_run_admission_is_bounded_per_session` launches `WORKFLOW_MAX_ACTIVE_RUNS_PER_SESSION` (= 4)
workflow runs, waits for each run's spawn event, and then asserts that a fifth launch is refused.
Before that assertion the only unbounded await was the event wait itself; `cancel_all_and_drain`
takes a `Duration` and bounds each receiver with `tokio::time::timeout_at`, so it cannot hang.

The event itself cannot be lost in transit. The production sender posts to an unbounded channel
before it awaits anything (`crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/implementations/grok_build/task/backend.rs:512`),
and a closed channel surfaces as `Ok(None)` rather than silence. So the wait can only stall if the
run task never reaches `agent()` -- which is precisely the state a saturated or degraded runner can
produce, and precisely the state a dropped event produces deterministically.

== 4. Mechanisms ruled out

Two candidate mechanisms were checked and cleared, and the stall's true cause is recorded as
unresolved rather than guessed at:

* `acquire_agent_slot` (`session/workflow/host_service.rs:376`) parks an agent on a semaphore when
  no slot is free, and it is reached before the spawn event is sent. But the semaphore is per run
  (`Semaphore::new(max_concurrent_agents)`, default 32, clamped to machine parallelism with a floor
  of 2), and this test needs one permit per run. It cannot starve here.
* `cancel_all_and_drain` was already bounded by `timeout_at(deadline, done)`.

Locally the test is not slow: the module suite finishes in a fifth of a second and 25 consecutive
rounds of this test passed at roughly 0.08 s each. What is not in doubt is the missing deadline.

== 5. The bounded helper, and the precedent it follows

    /// A spawn event that never arrives has to fail the test, not the job. The only
    /// ceiling on the CI job is its `timeout-minutes`, so an unbounded `recv()`
    /// trades a named failure for a silent wait out the whole job budget; `what`
    /// says which awaited spawn the deadline fired on.
    async fn recv_spawn(
        rx: &mut SubagentEventRx,
        what: &str,
    ) -> xai_grok_tools::implementations::grok_build::task::types::SubagentSpawnRequest {
        use xai_grok_tools::implementations::grok_build::task::types::SubagentEvent;
        match tokio::time::timeout(std::time::Duration::from_secs(2), rx.recv()).await {
            Ok(Some(SubagentEvent::Spawn(req))) => req,
            Ok(Some(_)) => panic!("{what}: expected spawn, got a non-spawn event"),
            Ok(None) => panic!("{what}: expected spawn, channel closed"),
            Err(_) => panic!("{what}: no spawn event within 2s"),
        }
    }

The 2-second bound is not a guess and not new. Two tests that already used it passed in the very run
that hung -- `parallel_panel_respects_concurrency_cap` receives several spawn events back to back
under that same bound, and `cancel_drops_queued_spawns_before_coordinator` likewise:

    $ grep -c "workflow::manager::tests::parallel_panel_respects_concurrency_cap \.\.\. ok" b21-ci-job.log
    1
    $ grep -c "workflow::manager::tests::cancel_drops_queued_spawns_before_coordinator \.\.\. ok" b21-ci-job.log
    1

So on that runner, in that degraded run, 2 seconds was demonstrably enough for this event type. The
shape also already exists twice elsewhere in the workspace: `recv_spawn` itself, and
`next_event` in `crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/implementations/grok_build/scheduler/actor.rs:1946`
(3 seconds). A third spelling of "wait forever" was the outlier, not the new bound.

== 6. Red baseline: the pre-fix source really does hang

Before crediting the fix, the failure was reproduced on purpose. The test fixture's relay task is the
thing that forwards spawn events to the test; making it drop `Spawn` events stands in for "the run
task never got there". Mutating only the pre-fix source and running only the test binary (compilation
untimed, `timeout` around the run only):

    $ timeout 30 xai_grok_shell-08161f36e816855b session::workflow::manager::tests::active_run_admission_is_bounded_per_session --exact --nocapture
    # exit=124 (124 == killed at the 30s bound)

    running 1 test

That is the whole transcript. `running 1 test`, then 30 seconds of nothing, then SIGKILL -- the same
shape as the CI log at a 60-minute scale. The unmutated baseline in the same harness passed in 0.13 s,
so the silence is the mutation, not the harness.

== 7. The matrix

Six mutants, each applied to a copy, compiled untimed, run under a 30-second bound, then restored and
verified with `cmp` against the saved base.

| cell | mutation | expected | result |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `B0` | nothing (baseline) | pass | pass in 0.13s |
| `H1` | pre-fix source + relay drops `Spawn` | hang | hang in 30.01s |
| `F1` | fixed source + relay drops `Spawn` | fail, names the spawn | fail in 2.06s |
| `F2` | as `F1`, plus the call site's label renamed | fail, names `LABELPROBE` | fail in 2.06s |
| `F3` | relay closes the channel instead | fail, says "channel closed" | fail in 0.09s |
| `F4` | as `F1`, but the deadline branch becomes `pending()` | hang | hang in 30.01s |
| `F5` | admission ceiling rewritten to `>= 1000` | fail | fail in 0.79s |

`F1`'s failure, verbatim:

    test session::workflow::manager::tests::active_run_admission_is_bounded_per_session ... FAILED

    test result: FAILED. 0 passed; 1 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 6931 filtered out; finished in 2.02s

    thread 'session::workflow::manager::tests::active_run_admission_is_bounded_per_session' (3376847) panicked at crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/src/session/workflow/manager.rs:948:23:
    spawn event: no spawn event within 2s

`F4` is the cell that keeps the refactor from claiming credit it did not earn: with every part of the
rewrite in place and only the deadline replaced by `std::future::pending()`, the test hangs again. The
deadline is the load-bearing part; routing through a shared helper is what makes it cheap to have.

`F5` proves the rewrite did not weaken the test. With the ceiling gone, the fifth launch is accepted
and the "must be refused" line goes red on its own:

    thread 'session::workflow::manager::tests::active_run_admission_is_bounded_per_session' (3373625) panicked at crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/src/session/workflow/manager.rs:1598:14:
    called `Result::unwrap_err()` on an `Ok` value: ("wf_01a10aa94c0c7a02a4b0b912bac2a919", Receiver { .. })

== 8. Two survivors that belonged to the expectation table, not the code

The first full pass reported `matrix: 6 mutants, 4 caught, 2 survived`. Both survivors were my
expectations being wrong, and both are recorded because the failure mode is easy to repeat:

* `F2` renamed `recv_spawn(&mut subagent_rx, "spawn event")` to `"LABELPROBE"`, but three converted
  sites share that label text, so `replace(.., 1)` renamed a different call site and the assertion
  test still printed its own (correct) label. Re-anchored on the admission test's own line
  `spawned.push(recv_spawn(&mut subagent_rx, "spawn event").await);`, the cell is caught.
* `F5` expected the needle `called \`Result::unwrap()\` on an \`Ok\` value` while the actual panic came
  from `unwrap_err()`. The test was red all along; the string comparison said otherwise.

An earlier attempt at the same matrix reported four hangs that were not hangs: `timeout` had been put
around `cargo test`, so it also bounded the roughly 180-second compile and every cell was killed
before the test started. Timing may only wrap the test binary.

== 9. Readings

Module suite on the rewritten source:

    $ RUST_MIN_STACK=16777216 cargo test -p xai-grok-shell --lib session::workflow::manager::tests
    test result: ok. 24 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 6908 filtered out; finished in 0.22s

Inventory of the anti-pattern `.recv().await.expect(` in test code, workspace-wide: 84 sites before
this batch, 69 after, `workflow/manager.rs` at zero. The 69 are spread over other crates' test
modules; the largest clusters are `grok_build/task/coordinator_tests.rs` (18),
`xai-file-utils/src/queue_tests.rs` (10) and `grok_build/ask_user_question/mod.rs` (6). Every one of
the 69 was checked to sit inside a test function, so this is test-side debt, not a production wait.

== 10. CI verdicts and how to reproduce

`a8274d9f` (run 37259667663): `cancelled` at the 60-minute ceiling, root cause this batch's unbounded
wait, recorded above.

`f0f7f551` (run 37268644414, the batch that introduced the shared lock-recovery trait): `success`,
all eight jobs -- `rust check / clippy / test`, `GUI browser E2E`, `GUI engine, Web host and
frontend`, `installer integrity labs`, `npm package scripts`, `docs localization`, `secret scan`,
`workflows present`. The workflow test did not hang on that run, which is consistent with a
load-sensitive stall rather than a deterministic deadlock, and is the reason the remaining root cause
is left open in TODO.md instead of being closed with this change.

    $ git rev-parse --short HEAD
    $ RUST_MIN_STACK=16777216 cargo test -p xai-grok-shell --lib session::workflow::manager::tests
    $ python3 {SCRATCH}/b23/matrix.py
    B0 unmutated baseline: pass in 0.13s
    H1-orig-drop: expected hang, got hang in 30.01s -- CAUGHT
    F1-fixed-drop: expected fail, got fail in 2.06s -- CAUGHT needle found
    F3-closed: expected fail, got fail in 0.09s -- CAUGHT needle found
    F4-nodeadline: expected hang, got hang in 30.01s -- CAUGHT
    $ python3 {SCRATCH}/b23/matrix.py --only=F2-label,F5-admission-off
    matrix: 2 mutants, 2 caught, 0 survived, survivors []
    $ python3 {SCRATCH}/b23/capture.py

`{SCRATCH}` holds the throwaway harness (`matrix.py`, `capture.py`, the pre-fix and post-fix copies of
`manager.rs` used as matrix bases); the mutations themselves are applied to a copy and every cell
restores the source and checks it with `cmp`.
